  "beats": [
    {
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "On Linux, Jo traces a shell launching a child command. The excerpt is illustrative; shells and libraries choose different system calls.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "term.type",
          "id": "trace",
          "cmd": "strace -f -e trace=process -- sh -c 'sleep 1 & wait'",
          "out": "Illustrative launch; syscall sequence varies"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "rookie",
      "text": "I see a child creation call and then execve. Predict this: after execve replaces the program image, does the child's process identifier change?",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "tree",
          "id": "question",
          "nodes": [
            {
              "id": "shell",
              "label": "shell · PID 410"
            },
            {
              "id": "child",
              "label": "child · PID 411",
              "parent": "shell"
            }
          ],
          "path": [
            "shell",
            "child"
          ]
        },
        {
          "do": "define",
          "term": "PID"
        },
        {
          "do": "sketch.note",
          "id": "reveal-note",
          "text": "Child PID 411"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "senior",
      "text": "It stays the same. Exec replaces the current process image with a new program; it does not create a second process. The child keeps its identifier.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "tree",
          "id": "exec",
          "nodes": [
            {
              "id": "shell",
              "label": "shell"
            },
            {
              "id": "image",
              "label": "PID 411 · new image",
              "parent": "shell"
            }
          ],
          "path": [
            "shell",
            "image"
          ]
        },
        {
          "do": "sketch.note",
          "id": "reveal-note",
          "text": "Same PID after exec"
        }
      ],
      "hold_ms": 300
    },
    {
      "speaker": "rookie",
      "text": "Then what does fork add before exec, and why might vfork look different in the trace?",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "sketch.note",
          "id": "next",
          "text": "Creation and replacement are separate steps"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "senior",
      "text": "Fork creates a child with a separate address space, commonly using copy-on-write. Vfork temporarily shares memory and suspends the parent thread until the child execs or exits.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "seq.diagram",
          "id": "fork",
          "actors": [
            "Parent",
            "Child"
          ],
          "messages": [
            {
              "from": "Parent",
              "to": "Child",
              "label": "fork: create child"
            },
            {
              "from": "Child",
              "to": "Parent",
              "label": "copy-on-write address space",
              "reply": true
            },
            {
              "from": "Parent",
              "to": "Child",
              "label": "vfork: parent waits"
            },
            {
              "from": "Child",
              "to": "Parent",
              "label": "exec or exit releases wait",
              "reply": true
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "rookie",
      "text": "And clone flags tell me which resources are shared, while posix_spawn gives callers a smaller launch routine?",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "sketch.note",
          "id": "question2",
          "text": "Which resources, and which setup steps?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "senior",
      "text": "Right. Linux clone flags select sharing such as memory or file descriptors. The Portable Operating System Interface, POSIX, spawn routine packages process creation, file actions, attributes and exec; libc details vary.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "code.editor",
          "id": "choices",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "clone: choose sharing flags",
            "CLONE_VM  → shared memory",
            "CLONE_FILES → shared descriptor table",
            "POSIX spawn: file actions + exec",
            "glibc 2.24+: clone with VM + VFORK"
          ]
        },
        {
          "do": "define",
          "term": "POSIX"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "rookie",
      "text": "I will label creation and replacement separately in my trace. One library call can use different syscalls; replacing the program does not create another process.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
